    "lawCode": "Amd §§555, 556 & 558, R & SS L",
    "lawSection": "Retirement and Social Security Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S5655\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\nAn act\nto amend the retirement and social security law, in relation to\ndisability retirement applications made by or on behalf of certain\ndeputy sheriffs\n\nPURPOSE OF THE BILL:\nTo provide a fair pension benefit for Sheriff's Deputies who are\ninjured performing law enforcement duty but who choose not to\nimmediately retire as a result of their injury because they are\neither attempting to come back to work or because it is uncertain\nwhether or not they will be able to go back to work.\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\nSection 1: Amends subdivision aa of section 555 of the retirement and\nsocial security law to provide that an application for disability\nretirement shall not be disapproved on the basis of a deputy sheriff\nhaving failed to engage directly in criminal law enforcement\nactivities that aggregate fifty percent of a deputy sheriff's service\nduring a period preceding the filing of the application, provided the\nfailure to do so was the result of the disability alleged in the\napplication and further provided the deputy sheriff was certified as\nso engaged in criminal law enforcement activities by the county\nsheriff for the calendar year preceding the onset of the disability.\n\nSection 2: Amends subdivision a of section 556 of the retirement and\nsocial security law to provide that an application for disability\nretirement shall not be disapproved on the basis of a deputy sheriff\nhaving failed to engage directly in criminal law enforcement\nactivities that aggregate fifty percent of a deputy sheriff's service\nduring a period preceding the filing of the application provided the\nfailure to do so was the result of the disability alleged in the\napplication and further provided the deputy sheriff was certified as\nso engaged in criminal law enforcement activities by the county\nsheriff for the calendar year preceding the onset of the disability.\n\nSection 3: Amends subdivision b of section 558 of the retirement and\nsocial security law to provide that an application for disability\nretirement shall not be disapproved on the basis of a deputy sheriff\nhaving failed to engage directly in criminal law enforcement\nactivities that aggregate fifty percent of a deputy sheriff's service\nduring a period preceding the filing of the application provided the\nfailure to do so was the result of the disability alleged in the\napplication and further provided the deputy sheriff was certified as\nso engaged in criminal law enforcement activities by the county\nsheriff for the calendar year preceding the onset of the disability.\n\nSection 4: Effective date.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\nDeputy Sheriff's injured in the line of duty performing law\nenforcement activities should not be penalized in their pension\nbecause they did not immediately file for permanent retirement. This\nlegislation was initiated because a Deputy Sheriff was injured in the\n\n\nline of duty. She was out of work on 207-C disability for a period of\napproximately two years (as a result of that injury). Thereafter, her\nemployer filed for her retirement. The Comptroller was forced to deny\nthe enhanced retirement benefits typically available to law\nenforcement from on the job injuries because the Deputy was not\nactively working in law enforcement the year the disability\nretirement was applied for (because she was recuperating from her\ninjury, but ultimately was unable to recuperate enough to engage\nagain in law enforcement work).\n\nThe fact that a Deputy continues to try and recover, and that\nattempted recovery takes more than a year, before ultimately the\nDeputy or his/her employer seek to officially retire the Deputy,\nshould not cause a substantially lower pension benefit (1/3) than the\nDeputy would have received (1/2 or 3/4) had the Deputy immediately\nfiled for a disability retirement.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\nNew Bill.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\nAccording to the New York State Comptroller, if this bill is enacted,\nthe number of affected members cannot be readily determined. The cost\nwould depend on the number of affected cases, as well as the type of\ndisability granted, the age, service, salary, plan and tier of the\naffected members. These costs would be borne by the State of New York\nand all participating employers in the New York State and Local\nEmployees' Retirement System.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately and shall apply to all\napplications filed pursuant to section 555, 556 or 558 of the\nretirement and social security law on and after July 1, 2009\n\n",
